  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Alice and Bob have NNN cards with them. The iii-th card has the number AiA_iAi​ written on its front, and BiB_iBi​ written on its back.\n\nIt's guaranteed that Ai≠AjA_i \\neq A_jAi​=Aj​ for i≠ji \\neq ji=j, i.e, all the AiA_iAi​ values are distinct.\n\nAll the cards are face-up, so only their AiA_iAi​ values are visible.\n\nAlice will choose exactly one card from the NNN cards, and remove it.\n\nAfter Alice has chosen her card, Bob will choose a card from the remaining N−1N-1N−1 cards.\n\nBob is greedy, and wants his card to have a large number on it. However, since he can only see the AiA_iAi​ values, he will always choose whichever card has the **maximum** AiA_iAi​ value among the remaining cards.\n\nIf Alice chooses card xxx, and Bob chooses card yyy, Alice will win if and only if\n\nmax⁡(Ax,Bx)&gt;max⁡(Ay,By)\\max(A_x, B_x) \\gt \\max(A_y, B_y)max(Ax​,Bx​)&gt;max(Ay​,By​)Is there a way for Alice to choose a card such that she wins?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the number of cards.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ — the numbers written on the fronts of the cards.\nThe third line contains NNN space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BNB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_NB1​,B2​,…,BN​ — the numbers written on the backs of the cards.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: `\"Yes\"` if Alice can win, and `\"No\"` otherwise (without quotes).\n\n\nEach character of the output may be printed in either uppercase or lowercase, i.e, the strings `No`, `NO`, `no`, and `nO` will all be treated as equivalent.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "2≤N≤2⋅1052 \\leq N \\leq 2\\cdot 10^52≤N≤2⋅105",
      "1≤Ai,Bi≤2⋅N1 \\leq A_i, B_i \\leq 2\\cdot N1≤Ai​,Bi​≤2⋅N",
      "Ai≠AjA_i \\neq A_jAi​=Aj​ for i≠ji \\neq ji=j",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 2⋅1052\\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n2\n1 3\n3 4\n2\n1 3\n3 1\n3\n3 4 5\n6 6 6\n4\n4 3 1 5\n6 5 7 2",
        "output": "Yes\nNo\nNo\nYes",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Alice can choose the second card, forcing Bob to choose the first.\nmax⁡(A2,B2)=4>3=max⁡(A1,B1)\\max(A_2, B_2) = 4 \\gt 3 = \\max(A_1, B_1)max(A2​,B2​)=4>3=max(A1​,B1​), so Alice wins.\nTest case 222: No matter which card Alice chooses, max⁡(Ai,Bi)=3\\max(A_i, B_i) = 3max(Ai​,Bi​)=3.\nAlice needs the value to be strictly greater to win; since they're equal here, she can't win.\nTest case 444: Alice can choose the first card, and Bob will choose the last (since 555 is the largest remaining AiA_iAi​ value).\nmax⁡(A1,B1)=6>5=max⁡(A4,B4)\\max(A_1, B_1) = 6 \\gt 5 = \\max(A_4, B_4)max(A1​,B1​)=6>5=max(A4​,B4​), so Alice wins.\nNote that Alice wouldn't have won if Bob chose the third card, since max⁡(A3,B3)=7>6\\max(A_3, B_3) = 7 \\gt 6max(A3​,B3​)=7>6.\nHowever, A3=1A_3 = 1A3​=1 isn't the largest remaining AiA_iAi​ value after Alice's choice, so Bob won't choose this card."
      }
    ]
  },
